;; DRIVER-LL Phase 4, sub-phase 4a: the sequencer.
;;
;; The stage loop of scripts/rfc_to_implementation.py:1915-2015 ported as
;; def-shell orchestration around two proved cores: skip.may-skip decides
;; resumption and stage.record-outcome classifies a halt. Neither decision is
;; re-implemented here, which is Phase 3's standing rule (spine.llmll:12-16).
;;
;; NO STAGE BODIES. Proposal section 9's 4a row. A stage that runs writes a
;; stub byte-string to each artifact it declares and nothing else; the eleven
;; resume and outcome transitions of section 2.3 are decided over those stubs
;; exactly as they would be over real artifacts, because the resume gate reads
;; digests and a completion record and never the content.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; SECTION 7 STATEMENT 1 OF 2 [DISCLOSURE skip.may-skip]
;;
;; may-skip is proved over four booleans. Three of the four are computed HERE,
;; in unproved shell code, and the proof says nothing about whether they are
;; the right booleans. Four shell-side decisions, one more than proposal Rev 7
;; lists:
;;
;;   1. A recorded digest of None arrives as "" (manifest.recorded-digest) and
;;      compares unequal to every hex string, so an artifact whose integrity
;;      was never recorded is a MISMATCH. Mirrors the reference at :1941.
;;
;;   2. An unknown `status` string parses to manifest-complete = false. That is
;;      conformant with driver-spec section 5:189-191 and it is a shell reading
;;      of a spec sentence, not a proved mapping.
;;
;;   3. artifacts-present and digests-match are BOTH derived from one
;;      wasi.fs.sha256 per declared artifact. MEASURED at v0.14.84: sha256 on a
;;      missing path yields RErr and on a present one RText hex, so presence and
;;      digest collapse into one command distinguished by CONSTRUCTOR. Every
;;      RErr is read as "absent", so an unreadable but present artifact is
;;      indistinguishable from a missing one at the proved boundary. The
;;      collapse is conservative: it can only force a re-run, never permit a
;;      skip, so it cannot violate [S5-SKIP]. It violates no clause and can
;;      still be wrong, which is why it is disclosed rather than argued away.
;;
;;   4. A response arm that is neither RText nor RErr (RNone, RCode, RList)
;;      is also read as "absent". No wasi.fs.sha256 produces those arms, so the
;;      branch is unreachable rather than merely untaken, and it fails in the
;;      same safe direction.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; SECTION 7 STATEMENT 2 OF 2 [DISCLOSURE stage.record-outcome]
;;
;; record-outcome proves Outcome -> Status over all four constructors. WHICH
;; constructor a site builds is chosen here, in unproved shell code, and it is
;; the discrimination the Python driver lost (proposal section 3.4).
;;
;; At 4a the choice is FULLY ENUMERABLE and narrower than what 4b onward will
;; owe: two flags, --halt-at naming a stage key and --halt-kind naming a
;; constructor, and nothing else in this module constructs a halt. There is no
;; 46-site table to audit yet because there are no stage bodies. When the
;; bodies land, this statement widens to proposal section 3.5's rule plus
;; section 3.6's artifact-state axis, and the gap inventory must not read as
;; though 4a had closed something it deferred.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; THE FAULT INJECTOR, AND WHY 4a NEEDS ONE.
;;
;; Section 9 demands all four Outcome arms with no stage bodies. With no
;; bodies only Finished has a producer, so ConditionUnmet, Errored and
;; PartialThenHalt need a declared injector, which is this port's counterpart
;; to the rig's STUB_MODE. It is 4a-ONLY SCAFFOLDING and its retirement is
;; scheduled: 4b retires Errored (a delegated output absent or malformed), 4c
;; through 4e retire ConditionUnmet at the gates, and 4f retires
;; PartialThenHalt at stage O's perturbation-omission check per section 6.2.
;; The injected halt's `clause` is "4a-injector" and is NOT a driver-spec
;; clause; a real clause identifier arrives with the stage that owns it.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; THE PROCESS BOUNDARY (PROC-BOUNDARY-1, shipped v0.14.84).
;;
;; argv arrives as :init's command on the RList arm through RC-3, and the exit
;; status is the :status projection. Both halves are used as specified. One
;; measured LIMIT, and it is a finding against the proposal's section 4.4
;; rather than a defect:
;;
;;   the range refinement {v : int | 0 <= v <= 255} is proved only where the
;;   function :status names is BODY-FAITHFUL, and a projection out of a
;;   structured state is not. MEASURED at v0.14.84: a def whose body applies
;;   `first` to a pair falls back, for (int, int) as much as for this state
;;   type, so drv-status is contract-checked rather than proved. The
;;   proc_boundary.llmll fixture takes `[s: int]` and is the provable case; no
;;   driver with a product state is.
;;
;; What carries the weight instead is exit-code below, which IS body-faithful
;; and whose [EXIT-NOVACUOUS] post is the eliminative one: a run that did not
;; complete cannot exit 0. drv-status then clamps, so a state carrying 256
;; exits 255 rather than exiting 0 and reporting success.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; TWO OUT-OF-STAGE HALTS CARRY NO STAGE STATUS.
;;
;; A corrupt MANIFEST.json (proposal section 10 cases 16 to 18) is decided
;; before any stage exists, so no Status attaches to it and record-outcome is
;; not called. The exit code is the literal 2, mirroring the reference's
;; top-level `except Halt` handler (rfc_to_implementation.py:2027-2032), and
;; the manifest is NOT written. Calling exit-code with a manufactured Stopped
;; would assert a stage disposition that driver-spec section 4 does not reach.

(module sequencer
  (import wasi.io   (capability stdout :deterministic false))
  ;; CAP-1-REAL: the verb and the path are documentation, not a bound.
  (import wasi.fs   (capability read-write "/"))
  ;; wasi.proc.args shares the wasi.proc namespace with wasi.proc.run
  ;; (extractWasiNamespace takes the first two segments), so this clause grants
  ;; the argument vector.
  (import wasi.proc (capability exec :deterministic false))

  (import stage)    (open stage)
  (import skip)     (open skip)
  (import registry) (open registry)
  (import manifest) (open manifest)

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; The proved centre this module adds
  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------

  ;; The process exit status of a run whose disposition is `s`. Body-faithful,
  ;; so the range and the three mappings are proved rather than asserted.
  ;;
  ;; 0 / 2 / 3 mirror the reference's three return paths (:1978, :1994, :2015
  ;; and the final :2019). Pending is 1 and is unreachable: nothing here builds
  ;; Pending, and [S4-ATTEMPTED] forbids record-outcome from returning it.
  (def exit-code [s: Status] -> int

    (post (and (>= result 0) (<= result 255))
      :source "[EXIT-RANGE] PROC-BOUNDARY-1 sec 4.4 - a status outside 0..255 is truncated by POSIX, so 256 would exit 0 and report success")

    (post (=> (= s Complete) (= result 0))
      :source "[EXIT-COMPLETE] a run whose stages all completed exits 0")

    (post (=> (= s Stopped) (= result 2))
      :source "[EXIT-STOPPED] driver-spec.txt sec 4 - a stage that halted on a defined condition exits 2, as rfc_to_implementation.py:1978 does")

    (post (=> (= s Failed) (= result 3))
      :source "[EXIT-FAILED] driver-spec.txt sec 4 - a stage that halted for any other reason exits 3, as rfc_to_implementation.py:1994 does")

    ;; The eliminative one. A body that returned 0 for a halted run passes
    ;; every implication above that does not mention it and is refuted here.
    (post (=> (not (= s Complete)) (> result 0))
      :source "[EXIT-NOVACUOUS] a run that did not complete MUST NOT report success")

    (match s
      ((Complete) 0)
      ((Stopped)  2)
      ((Failed)   3)
      ((Pending)  1)))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; State
  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------

  ;; workdir, (only-selection, (halt-at, (halt-kind, forced))). The selection
  ;; travels as the raw comma-separated string rather than as a list, because
  ;; an empty string has to mean "every stage" and an empty list would mean
  ;; "no stage": string-split with an empty subject yields [""], measured.
  (type Cfg (| Cfg (string, (string, (string, (string, bool))))))

  ;; The run-common record of proposal section 4: config, the live manifest,
  ;; and the selected stage indices in registry order.
  (type Run (| Run (Cfg, (Json, list[int]))))

  ;; Proposal section 4's sum over phases. Each arm names the RESPONSE it is
  ;; about to receive, since the console loop delivers the response to the
  ;; command issued on entry to the arm.
  ;;
  ;;   Boot 0     r is the argument vector
  ;;   Boot 1     r is the MANIFEST.json read
  ;;   Digest     r is wasi.fs.sha256 of declared artifact j, on the resume probe
  ;;   Started    r is the clock reading taken when the stage started
  ;;   Made       r is the write of artifact j
  ;;   Summed     r is wasi.fs.sha256 of artifact j, for the outputs map
  ;;   Stamped    r is the clock reading taken at record time
  ;;   Recorded   r is the manifest write
  ;;   Ending     r is the last report; the next state is terminal
  ;;   Done       terminal; :status reads the code out of this arm
  ;;
  ;; Ending exists because of RC-4: done? is evaluated on the state a step
  ;; RETURNS and the terminating step's command is DROPPED, so a state that
  ;; must still say something cannot be terminal. Recorded needs no such arm,
  ;; the manifest write having already been performed on entry to it.
  (type Phase
    (| Boot     int)
    (| Digest   (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string)))))
    (| Started  int)
    (| Made     (int, (int, int)))
    (| Summed   (int, (int, (int, Json))))
    (| Stamped  (int, (int, Json)))
    (| Recorded (int, int))
    (| Ending   int)
    (| Done     int))

  ;; ---- accessors, written once ---------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; Proposal section 13 risk 6: a wrong projection over a pair chain
  ;; TYPECHECKS. Every projection in this module is in one of the functions
  ;; below and no use site writes a bare (first (second ...)).

  (def-shell cfg-workdir [c: Cfg] -> string
    (match c ((Cfg p) (first p))))
  (def-shell cfg-only [c: Cfg] -> string
    (match c ((Cfg p) (first (second p)))))
  (def-shell cfg-halt-at [c: Cfg] -> string
    (match c ((Cfg p) (first (second (second p))))))
  (def-shell cfg-halt-kind [c: Cfg] -> string
    (match c ((Cfg p) (first (second (second (second p)))))))
  (def-shell cfg-forced [c: Cfg] -> bool
    (match c ((Cfg p) (second (second (second (second p)))))))

  (def-shell cfg-of [rn: Run] -> Cfg
    (match rn ((Run p) (first p))))
  (def-shell man-of [rn: Run] -> Json
    (match rn ((Run p) (first (second p)))))
  (def-shell sel-of [rn: Run] -> list[int]
    (match rn ((Run p) (second (second p)))))

  (def-shell with-cfg [rn: Run c: Cfg] -> Run
    (Run (pair c (pair (man-of rn) (sel-of rn)))))
  (def-shell with-man [rn: Run m: Json] -> Run
    (Run (pair (cfg-of rn) (pair m (sel-of rn)))))
  (def-shell with-sel [rn: Run xs: list[int]] -> Run
    (Run (pair (cfg-of rn) (pair (man-of rn) xs))))

  (def-shell dg-k [d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string))))] -> int
    (first d))
  (def-shell dg-j [d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string))))] -> int
    (first (second d)))
  (def-shell dg-present [d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string))))] -> bool
    (first (second (second d))))
  (def-shell dg-match [d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string))))] -> bool
    (first (second (second (second d)))))
  (def-shell dg-names [d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string))))] -> string
    (second (second (second (second d)))))
  (def-shell mk-dg [k: int j: int present: bool matched: bool names: string] -> Phase
    (Digest (pair k (pair j (pair present (pair matched names))))))

  (def-shell md-k [d: (int, (int, int))] -> int (first d))
  (def-shell md-j [d: (int, (int, int))] -> int (first (second d)))
  (def-shell md-start [d: (int, (int, int))] -> int (second (second d)))
  (def-shell mk-md [k: int j: int start: int] -> Phase
    (Made (pair k (pair j start))))

  (def-shell sm-k [d: (int, (int, (int, Json)))] -> int (first d))
  (def-shell sm-j [d: (int, (int, (int, Json)))] -> int (first (second d)))
  (def-shell sm-start [d: (int, (int, (int, Json)))] -> int (first (second (second d))))
  (def-shell sm-outs [d: (int, (int, (int, Json)))] -> Json (second (second (second d))))
  (def-shell mk-sm [k: int j: int start: int outs: Json] -> Phase
    (Summed (pair k (pair j (pair start outs)))))

  (def-shell st-k [d: (int, (int, Json))] -> int (first d))
  (def-shell st-start [d: (int, (int, Json))] -> int (first (second d)))
  (def-shell st-outs [d: (int, (int, Json))] -> Json (second (second d)))
  (def-shell mk-st [k: int start: int outs: Json] -> Phase
    (Stamped (pair k (pair start outs))))

  (def-shell rc-k [d: (int, int)] -> int (first d))
  (def-shell rc-code [d: (int, int)] -> int (second d))
  (def-shell mk-rc [k: int code: int] -> Phase
    (Recorded (pair k code)))

  (def-shell run-of [s: (Run, Phase)] -> Run (first s))
  (def-shell phase-of [s: (Run, Phase)] -> Phase (second s))
  (def-shell go [rn: Run p: Phase c: Command] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (pair (pair rn p) c))

  ;; ---- argv ----------------------------------------------------------

  ;; The element following `f`, or "". One left-to-right fold carrying
  ;; (value-so-far, take-the-next-one); a repeated flag keeps the FIRST value,
  ;; which is the opposite of argparse and is stated because nothing enforces
  ;; it.
  (def-shell flag-value [as: list[string] f: string] -> string
    (first (list-fold as (pair "" false)
             (fn [acc: (string, bool) a: string]
               (if (second acc)
                   (pair a false)
                   (if (= a f) (pair (first acc) true) acc))))))

  (def-shell dflt [s: string d: string] -> string
    (if (string-empty? s) d s))

  (def-shell parse-cfg [as: list[string]] -> Cfg
    (Cfg (pair (dflt (flag-value as "--workdir") ".")
               (pair (flag-value as "--only")
                     (pair (flag-value as "--halt-at")
                           (pair (flag-value as "--halt-kind")
                                 (list-contains as "--force")))))))

  (def-shell argv-of [r: Response] -> list[string]
    (match r
      ((RList as) as)
      ((RNone)    (list-empty))
      ((RText _)  (list-empty))
      ((RCode _)  (list-empty))
      ((RErr _)   (list-empty))))

  ;; MEASURED at v0.14.84, and the spec does not say which way round it goes:
  ;; string-split takes the SEPARATOR FIRST. LLMLL.md:2433 gives the type as
  ;; `string string -> list[string]` with no argument names, the generated
  ;; body is `string_split sep str` (CodegenHs.hs:359-366), and there is not
  ;; one call site in the tree to read it off. Getting it backwards splits the
  ;; separator by the subject and yields [","], which type-checks and passes
  ;; every check-time gate.
  (def-shell split-on [sep: string s: string] -> list[string]
    (string-split sep s))

  (def-shell key-selected? [c: Cfg i: int] -> bool
    (if (string-empty? (cfg-only c))
        true
        (list-contains (split-on "," (cfg-only c)) (stage-key i))))

  (def-shell sel-indices [c: Cfg] -> list[int]
    (list-filter (range 0 (stage-count)) (fn [i: int] (key-selected? c i))))

  (def-shell idx-at [rn: Run k: int] -> int
    (unwrap-or (list-nth (sel-of rn) k) 0))

  ;; ---- paths and stub bodies -----------------------------------------

  (def-shell manifest-path [c: Cfg] -> string
    (string-concat (cfg-workdir c) "/MANIFEST.json"))

  (def-shell art-path [c: Cfg i: int j: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many [(cfg-workdir c) "/" (stage-out i j)]))

  (def-shell art-dir [c: Cfg i: int j: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many [(cfg-workdir c) "/" (stage-out-dir i j)]))

  ;; No stage bodies at 4a. The content is per-artifact so that two stubs never
  ;; share a digest, which would make a cross-stage digest confusion invisible.
  (def-shell stub-body [i: int j: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["driver-ll 4a stub\nstage=" (stage-key i) "\nartifact=" (stage-out i j) "\n"]))

  ;; ---- response projections ------------------------------------------

  ;; "" for every arm but RText. wasi.fs.sha256 answers RText on a present path
  ;; and RErr on a missing one, measured; the other three arms are unreachable
  ;; and land on the same safe side. See [DISCLOSURE skip.may-skip] item 4.
  (def-shell hex-of [r: Response] -> string
    (match r
      ((RText t)  t)
      ((RErr _)   "")
      ((RNone)    "")
      ((RCode _)  "")
      ((RList _)  "")))

  (def-shell ns-of [r: Response] -> int
    (match r
      ((RCode n)  n)
      ((RText _)  0)
      ((RErr _)   0)
      ((RNone)    0)
      ((RList _)  0)))

  ;; Whole seconds. json-of-int is the only numeric injection, so the field
  ;; cannot carry the reference's one-decimal float; see manifest.llmll's
  ;; header. Negative is impossible from a monotonic clock and is clamped
  ;; anyway, since a negative duration in an artifact is worse than a zero.
  (def-shell secs-between [start: int end: int] -> int
    (if (< end start) 0 (/ (- end start) 1000000000)))

  ;; ---- the injector --------------------------------------------------

  (def-shell injected-kind [rn: Run k: int] -> string
    (if (= (cfg-halt-at (cfg-of rn)) (stage-key (idx-at rn k)))
        (cfg-halt-kind (cfg-of rn))
        ""))

  (def-shell halts-pre? [rn: Run k: int] -> bool
    (or (= (injected-kind rn k) "ConditionUnmet")
        (= (injected-kind rn k) "Errored")))

  (def-shell halts-post? [rn: Run k: int] -> bool
    (= (injected-kind rn k) "PartialThenHalt"))

  (def-shell injected-outcome [rn: Run k: int] -> Outcome
    (if (= (injected-kind rn k) "ConditionUnmet") ConditionUnmet
    (if (= (injected-kind rn k) "PartialThenHalt") PartialThenHalt
        Errored)))

  (def-shell halt-detail [rn: Run k: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["injected halt at stage " (stage-key (idx-at rn k))
       " (--halt-kind " (injected-kind rn k) ")"]))

  ;; ConditionUnmet and PartialThenHalt are the stopped path and carry a
  ;; clause; Errored is the failed path and carries none, mirroring the
  ;; reference's two halt-row shapes.
  (def-shell halt-clause [o: Outcome] -> string
    (match o
      ((ConditionUnmet)  "4a-injector")
      ((PartialThenHalt) "4a-injector")
      ((Errored)         "")
      ((Finished)        "")))

  ;; ---- report lines --------------------------------------------------

  (def-shell skip-line [i: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["[driver-ll] stage " (stage-key i) " (" (stage-name i)
       "): already complete, skipping"]))

  ;; The reference computes `mismatched` under `if recorded and artifacts`
  ;; (:1934), so a stage with a missing declared artifact reports NOTHING and
  ;; re-runs silently. That is transition T7, it is conformant (section 5
  ;; attaches a MUST-report to T6 and a SHOULD to T8 and is silent here), and
  ;; the same gate is written here rather than inherited by accident.
  (def-shell reason-line [i: int recorded: bool present: bool names: string] -> string
    (if (and recorded (and present (not (string-empty? names))))
        (string-concat-many
          ["[driver-ll] stage " (stage-key i)
           ": artifact(s) changed since this stage recorded completion ("
           names "); re-running. An artifact modified outside the protocol is "
           "not that stage's output.\n"])
        (if (and present (not recorded))
            (string-concat-many
              ["[driver-ll] stage " (stage-key i)
               ": artifacts present but no completion record (interrupted, or "
               "a previous attempt failed); re-running\n"])
            "")))

  (def-shell run-lines [i: int recorded: bool present: bool names: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      [(reason-line i recorded present names)
       "[driver-ll] stage " (stage-key i) " [" (stage-kind i) "] " (stage-name i)]))

  (def-shell halt-line [st: Status i: int detail: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["[driver-ll] " (if (= st Failed) "FAILED" "STOP") " at stage "
       (stage-key i) ": " detail]))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; The step machine
  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------

  ;; Issue stage k's first digest probe, or finish the run. Every site that
  ;; would advance to the next stage calls this, so "past the last stage" is
  ;; decided in one place.
  (def-shell enter [rn: Run k: int] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (>= k (list-length (sel-of rn)))
        (go rn (Ending 0)
            (wasi.io.stdout
              (string-concat "[driver-ll] done. manifest: " (manifest-path (cfg-of rn)))))
        (go rn (mk-dg k 0 true true "")
            (wasi.fs.sha256 (art-path (cfg-of rn) (idx-at rn k) 0)))))

  (def-shell start-run [rn: Run] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (enter (with-sel rn (sel-indices (cfg-of rn))) 0))

  ;; An ABSENT manifest is an empty one, not a fault: the reference returns
  ;; {"stages": {}} when the path does not exist (:246-247). Only a manifest
  ;; that IS there and cannot be used halts.
  (def-shell boot-read [rn: Run r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (match r
      ((RText body)
        (if (string-empty? (manifest-fault body))
            (start-run (with-man rn (manifest-of body)))
            (go rn (Ending 2)
                (wasi.io.stdout
                  (string-concat "[driver-ll] STOP: " (manifest-fault body))))))
      ((RErr _)   (start-run (with-man rn (empty-manifest))))
      ((RNone)    (start-run (with-man rn (empty-manifest))))
      ((RCode _)  (start-run (with-man rn (empty-manifest))))
      ((RList _)  (start-run (with-man rn (empty-manifest))))))

  (def-shell boot-step [rn: Run n: int r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (= n 0)
        (let [(c (parse-cfg (argv-of r)))]
          (go (with-cfg rn c) (Boot 1)
              (seq-commands (wasi.fs.mkdir (cfg-workdir c))
                            (wasi.fs.read (manifest-path c)))))
        (boot-read rn r)))

  ;; The resume decision. Nothing here re-implements it: the four booleans go
  ;; to skip.may-skip and its answer is the branch.
  ;;
  ;; `digests-match` IS THE REFERENCE'S `not mismatched`, AND THE GATE ON IT IS
  ;; NOT DECORATION. The reference computes `mismatched` under
  ;; `if recorded and artifacts` (:1934), so when a declared artifact is absent
  ;; the list is EMPTY and the skip is prevented by condition (b) alone. The
  ;; accumulated `matched` below cannot say that on its own: an absent artifact
  ;; answers RErr, hex is "", and "" differs from the recorded digest, so
  ;; presence and match fail TOGETHER and (c) masks (b).
  ;;
  ;; MEASURED, and this is why the gate is here rather than left implicit. With
  ;; the raw `matched`, passing a hardcoded `true` for artifacts-present left
  ;; ALL FIFTEEN cover scenarios green, T7 included: the cell proposal section
  ;; 2.3 defines as "condition (b) alone" was being decided by (c). With the
  ;; gate, the same perturbation turns T7 red. The decision is unchanged either
  ;; way, [S5-SKIP] holding in both, so this is about what the cover can
  ;; OBSERVE rather than about what the driver does.
  (def-shell decide [rn: Run k: int present: bool matched: bool names: string]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn k))]
      (let [(recorded (= (row-status (man-of rn) (stage-key i)) "complete"))]
        (if (may-skip recorded present
                      (if (and recorded present) matched true)
                      (cfg-forced (cfg-of rn)))
            (let [(e (enter rn (+ k 1)))]
              (pair (first e)
                    (seq-commands (wasi.io.stdout (skip-line i)) (second e))))
            (go rn (Started k)
                (seq-commands
                  (wasi.io.stdout (run-lines i recorded present names))
                  wasi.clock.monotonic))))))

  (def-shell digest-step [rn: Run d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string)))) r: Response]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn (dg-k d)))]
      (let [(hex (hex-of r))]
        (let [(want (recorded-digest (man-of rn) (stage-key i) (stage-out i (dg-j d))))]
          (let [(here (not (string-empty? hex)))]
            (let [(same (and here (= hex want)))]
              (let [(names (if same
                               (dg-names d)
                               (if here
                                   (string-concat-many
                                     [(dg-names d)
                                      (if (string-empty? (dg-names d)) "" ", ")
                                      (stage-out i (dg-j d))])
                                   (dg-names d))))]
                (if (< (+ (dg-j d) 1) (stage-out-count i))
                    (go rn (mk-dg (dg-k d) (+ (dg-j d) 1)
                                  (and (dg-present d) here)
                                  (and (dg-match d) same)
                                  names)
                        (wasi.fs.sha256 (art-path (cfg-of rn) i (+ (dg-j d) 1))))
                    (decide rn (dg-k d)
                            (and (dg-present d) here)
                            (and (dg-match d) same)
                            names)))))))))

  ;; The manifest write and the halt report leave in one seq-commands, so the
  ;; response the next arm receives is the WRITE's. RC-2 is discard-left.
  (def-shell halt-now [rn: Run k: int o: Outcome start: int] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn k))]
      (let [(st (record-outcome o))]
        (let [(m2 (put-row (man-of rn) (stage-key i)
                           (halt-row st o (halt-detail rn k) (halt-clause o))))]
          (go (with-man rn m2) (mk-rc k (exit-code st))
              (seq-commands
                (wasi.io.stdout (halt-line st i (halt-detail rn k)))
                (wasi.fs.write (manifest-path (cfg-of rn)) (json-serialize m2))))))))

  (def-shell write-cmd [rn: Run k: int j: int] -> Command
    (seq-commands (wasi.fs.mkdir (art-dir (cfg-of rn) (idx-at rn k) j))
                  (wasi.fs.write (art-path (cfg-of rn) (idx-at rn k) j)
                                 (stub-body (idx-at rn k) j))))

  (def-shell started-step [rn: Run k: int r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (halts-pre? rn k)
        (halt-now rn k (injected-outcome rn k) (ns-of r))
        (go rn (mk-md k 0 (ns-of r)) (write-cmd rn k 0))))

  (def-shell made-step [rn: Run d: (int, (int, int)) r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (< (+ (md-j d) 1) (stage-out-count (idx-at rn (md-k d))))
        (go rn (mk-md (md-k d) (+ (md-j d) 1) (md-start d))
            (write-cmd rn (md-k d) (+ (md-j d) 1)))
        (go rn (mk-sm (md-k d) 0 (md-start d) json-object)
            (wasi.fs.sha256 (art-path (cfg-of rn) (idx-at rn (md-k d)) 0)))))

  ;; The reference's outputs map is comprehended over the declared outputs THAT
  ;; EXIST (:2010-2011), so a missing artifact yields a SHORT map rather than a
  ;; null. An RErr here is that filter.
  (def-shell summed-step [rn: Run d: (int, (int, (int, Json))) r: Response]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn (sm-k d)))]
      (let [(outs (if (string-empty? (hex-of r))
                      (sm-outs d)
                      (jset (sm-outs d) (stage-out i (sm-j d))
                            (json-of-string (hex-of r)))))]
        (if (< (+ (sm-j d) 1) (stage-out-count i))
            (go rn (mk-sm (sm-k d) (+ (sm-j d) 1) (sm-start d) outs)
                (wasi.fs.sha256 (art-path (cfg-of rn) i (+ (sm-j d) 1))))
            (go rn (mk-st (sm-k d) (sm-start d) outs) wasi.clock.monotonic)))))

  (def-shell stamped-step [rn: Run d: (int, (int, Json)) r: Response]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (halts-post? rn (st-k d))
        (halt-now rn (st-k d) PartialThenHalt (st-start d))
        (let [(i (idx-at rn (st-k d)))]
          (let [(m2 (put-row (man-of rn) (stage-key i)
                             (complete-row (stage-kind i)
                                           (secs-between (st-start d) (ns-of r))
                                           (st-outs d))))]
            (go (with-man rn m2) (mk-rc (st-k d) 0)
                (wasi.fs.write (manifest-path (cfg-of rn)) (json-serialize m2)))))))

  ;; The manifest write has already happened on entry here, so a halt can go
  ;; straight to Done: the command this arm returns is the one RC-4 drops.
  (def-shell recorded-step [rn: Run d: (int, int)] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (= (rc-code d) 0)
        (enter rn (+ (rc-k d) 1))
        (go rn (Done (rc-code d)) (wasi.io.stdout ""))))

  (def-shell drv-init [] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (pair (pair (Run (pair (Cfg (pair "." (pair "" (pair "" (pair "" false)))))
                           (pair (empty-manifest) (list-empty))))
                (Boot 0))
          wasi.proc.args))

  (def-shell drv-step [s: (Run, Phase) input: string r: Response]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (match (phase-of s)
      ((Boot n)     (boot-step   (run-of s) n r))
      ((Digest d)   (digest-step (run-of s) d r))
      ((Started k)  (started-step (run-of s) k r))
      ((Made d)     (made-step   (run-of s) d r))
      ((Summed d)   (summed-step (run-of s) d r))
      ((Stamped d)  (stamped-step (run-of s) d r))
      ((Recorded d) (recorded-step (run-of s) d))
      ((Ending c)   (go (run-of s) (Done c) (wasi.io.stdout "")))
      ((Done c)     (go (run-of s) (Done c) (wasi.io.stdout "")))))

  (def-shell drv-done? [s: (Run, Phase)] -> bool
    (match (phase-of s)
      ((Boot _)     false)
      ((Digest _)   false)
      ((Started _)  false)
      ((Made _)     false)
      ((Summed _)   false)
      ((Stamped _)  false)
      ((Recorded _) false)
      ((Ending _)   false)
      ((Done _)     true)))

  (def-shell done-code [s: (Run, Phase)] -> int
    (match (phase-of s)
      ((Boot _)     1)
      ((Digest _)   1)
      ((Started _)  1)
      ((Made _)     1)
      ((Summed _)   1)
      ((Stamped _)  1)
      ((Recorded _) 1)
      ((Ending _)   1)
      ((Done c)     c)))

  ;; The terminal marker. It leaves through :on-done rather than through a
  ;; step, because RC-4 drops the terminating step's command and a halt report
  ;; that is constructed and not performed fails driver-spec section 4:139-143
  ;; SILENTLY. spine.llmll:673-679 records :on-done as unusable and is right
  ;; for an int state; section 4's sum carries the reason, so it becomes
  ;; available here.
  (def-shell drv-final [s: (Run, Phase)] -> Command
    (wasi.io.stdout
      (string-concat "[driver-ll] end status=" (int-to-string (done-code s)))))

  ;; :status. Contract-checked, not body-faithful; see the header. The clamp is
  ;; what makes the declared range true by construction rather than by
  ;; assertion, and it fails in the safe direction: 256 exits 255, not 0.
  ;;
  ;; EVERY ARM BINDS ITS PAYLOAD, including the eight that ignore it. A `_`
  ;; there is PWildcard, and isCoreBodySyntactic's payload-constructor leg
  ;; (Syntax.hs:810) admits only PConstructor n [PVar _], so a wildcard turns
  ;; the whole match into non-core syntax and the def is rejected at `check`.
  (def drv-status [s: (Run, Phase)] -> int
    (post (and (>= result 0) (<= result 255))
      :source "[EXIT-RANGE] PROC-BOUNDARY-1 sec 4.4 - the declared range on the function :status names")
    (match (second s)
      ((Boot b)     1)
      ((Digest d)   1)
      ((Started k)  1)
      ((Made m)     1)
      ((Summed u)   1)
      ((Stamped t)  1)
      ((Recorded v) 1)
      ((Ending e)   1)
      ((Done c)     (if (< c 0) 1 (if (> c 255) 255 c)))))

  (def-main
    :mode    console
    :init    (drv-init)
    :step    drv-step
    :done?   drv-done?
    :on-done drv-final
    :status  drv-status))
